text:
@Iceandtrees8787 As Claude reports the word ‘priceless’ is not literal, I use it as an expression of the English language;
Claude, Q-the use of the word priceless explained:

	A: •	Hyperbole: "Her advice was priceless" means extremely valuable.
	•	Idiom or colloquialism: "That's priceless!" means something is amusing or absurdly fitting. This sense is a conventional, informal extension rather than a literal reading.

Claude’s interpretation is as I intended: a hyperbolic expression. I’m not afraid of AI, I intend to explore how it can make me more productive to my company and society.

@Basel-d5z Sounds like your work takes less time and you don't have to read or write as many words.  
How do you verify the AI's audit results are accurate?
How do you know the words the AI left out with its summaries of the literature weren't important?

@Iceandtrees8787I have heard of 1 T, but where did the 4-10 trillion number come from? 

Also, making someone complete tasks faster is the best literal example of productivity… so idk what you meant by that. 

If AI can make employees complete tasks 20 minutes faster that is very much significant. For someone with a white collar job  who is payed say $60/h, that is $20 saved…. Spread that across hundreds of thousands of employees over years, and that is real productivity. (Also, I think 20 minutes is conservative and understates the efficiency AI provides).

@Iceandtrees8787then you’re 10 trillion estimate assumes the buildout goes on for an additional 11 years, that is not what the companies themselves say, but I guess you would know better than them.

And just to speak to your one point, yes economic productivity literally increases as one you make one task easier to do.
Ask the cotton gin and what that did for Early America.
